<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Territory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indian Territory was the land west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, set aside by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for Native American nations removed from the east under the Indian Removal Act of 1830. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, Chickasaw, Choctaw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> and Seminole were resettled there and governed their own republics. After the Civil War much land was taken for other tribes and then opened to settlers, and in 1907 the territory was merged into the state of Oklahoma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-territory.jpg" alt="Indian Territory" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Choctaw Nation · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Choctaw_Brigade.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Indian Territory</b> the Nation opened a public school system in 1841, launched the Cherokee Advocate in 1844 and opened its Male and Female Seminaries in 1851.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Texas joined the United States in 1845 the army built lines of forts, some Penateka settled on a reservation on the Clear Fork of the Brazos in 1854 and were moved to <b>Indian Territory</b> in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It was composed some time before 1862 by Wallace Willis and his daughter Minerva Willis, who were enslaved in the old <b>Indian Territory</b> by a Choctaw owner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/steal-away">Steal Away</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Swing Low, Sweet Chariot&quot; was probably composed sometime after 1865 by Wallace Willis and his daughter Minerva Willis, Choctaw Freedmen living in the old <b>Indian Territory</b>, near Hugo in what is now Choctaw County, Oklahoma.</blockquote><a href="/songs/swing-low-sweet-chariot">Swing Low, Sweet Chariot</a></li><li><blockquote>The first land run into the Unassigned Lands of the former <b>Indian Territory</b> started, with an estimated 50,000 people lined up for two million acres.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A group of 20 African Americans enslaved by the Cherokee escaped in <b>Indian Territory</b> and set out for Mexico, where slavery had been abolished in 1829.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
